### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
The claim contains a significant factual error: Nate McMurray served as Town Supervisor of Grand Island, **New York**, not Nebraska as stated by the claim author ([Spectrum News](https://spectrumlocalnews.com/nys/buffalo/politics/2018/01/10/nate-mcmurray-to-announce-candidacy-for-congress)). While a Grand Island does exist in Nebraska ([Visit Grand Island](https://visitgrandisland.com/plan-a-trip/things-to-do/nature-wildlife.html)), it is a separate and unrelated municipality. Regarding the tax vote assertion, evidence confirms that during McMurray's tenure, the Grand Island Town Board approved budgets that raised taxes—notably the 2020 budget, which increased the overall property tax levy by 3.47%, the general fund tax rate by 2.2%, and the water district tax rate by 34% ([Buffalo News via Wayback Machine](https://web.archive.org/web/20220322021746/https://buffalonews.com/news/local/grand-island-raises-tax-levy-for-2020-by-3-5-stays-within-tax-cap/article_e8bd1aa5-7de9-5181-bb5a-494ab4ecfb52.html)). However, the available sources do not specifically document McMurray's individual vote (yea or nay) on these measures, only attributing approval to "the Town Board" generally, and meeting minutes show the motions to adopt the budget were made by other council members ([ecode360](https://ecode360.com/documents/GR0053/public/466406368.pdf)). The claim is therefore misleading: the incorrect state misrepresents where McMurray served, and the specific assertion that he personally "voted to raise taxes" is not directly substantiated by the evidence, even though tax increases did occur during his tenure.